-\subsection{Parsing, Validation and Correction}
-Records are requested in batches of $b=30$. A local parser extracts the records from each response and fixes common syntax defects; if parsing still fails, the control layer sends one repair request that quotes the position of the error. The returned column names are then mapped back to $S$. The validator checks that every value has the type inferred for its column; optional rules can also bound numerical ranges or restrict categories, but they were off in our experiments. Failures are grouped by column and sent back to the LLM with one example of an invalid value per column, for at most $c=2$ correction rounds.
+\subsection{Deterministic Verifier (3)}
+The verifier turns a response into records that conform to $S$, in three steps. \emph{Parsing} extracts the records with a local parser that removes surrounding text and code fences, deletes trailing commas, inserts missing commas between records and closes a response truncated by the output limit. If parsing still fails, the control layer sends one repair request that quotes the position and the text around the error, at a low temperature so that the repair fixes the syntax without changing the content. \emph{Mapping} assigns the returned values to the columns of $S$: by position when the response has the expected number of columns, and otherwise by matching the returned column names to those of $S$, ignoring case and spacing; an invented column is dropped and a missing one is left empty. \emph{Validation} checks every value against the type that profiling inferred for its column (integer, real or categorical); optional rules can also bound numerical ranges or restrict categories, but they were off in our experiments except in the range-enforcement variant. The invalid values are grouped by column and sent back to the LLM with one example per column, for at most $c$ correction rounds, so that the model corrects only what is wrong instead of regenerating the batch.
 
