 \section{Conclusion}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
 %======================================================================
-Small healthcare datasets pose a dilemma for synthetic data generation: generators fitted to the available records can only recombine the patterns those records contain, whereas an LLM can add domain knowledge but returns text rather than a dataset. \system resolves the dilemma with a control layer that turns the responses of an LLM into complete synthetic datasets with the columns and types of the real data and a recorded cost, without training on the target data. On six public healthcare datasets, direct prompting never delivered the configured dataset, with LLMs of two providers and even with responses forced into a fixed format, while \system did at 1.9\% more cost, and with the strongest LLM it gave the largest and most stable F1 gain of five generators. Because repeated runs of the same pipeline vary as much as most design choices, we recommend that SDG for small data be evaluated on repeated runs. Future work will evaluate locally served LLMs, class-aware context selection, privacy filters and the deployment across healthcare facilities outlined in Section~\ref{sec:deployment}.
+Small healthcare datasets pose a dilemma for synthetic data generation: generators fitted to the available records can only recombine the patterns those records contain, whereas an LLM can add domain knowledge but returns text rather than a dataset. \system resolves the dilemma with a control layer that turns the responses of an LLM into complete synthetic datasets with the columns and types of the real data and a recorded cost, without training on the target data. On ten public healthcare datasets, direct prompting never delivered the configured dataset, with LLMs of two providers and even with responses forced into a fixed format, while \system did at 1.9\% more cost, and with the strongest LLM it gave the largest median F1 gain of fourteen generators without their large losses on single datasets. Because repeated runs of the same pipeline vary as much as most design choices, we recommend that SDG for small data be evaluated on repeated runs. Future work will evaluate locally served LLMs, class-aware context selection, privacy filters and the deployment across healthcare facilities outlined in Section~\ref{sec:deployment}.
 
